/** \page checkpointing Exact ODE checkpointing and replay

MFEM's checkpointing API reconstructs exact primal states for reverse-time
algorithms without requiring the complete forward trajectory to remain in
memory. The initial implementation is serial, synchronous, and supports the
fixed-step ForwardEulerSolver through ForwardEulerCheckpointAdapter.

\code{.unparsed}
TimeDependentOperator / ODESolver
                 |
                 v
       CheckpointController ------> application ReverseStepHandler
          |             |
          v             v
       Schedule      exact replay
                          |
                          v
            ODESolverCheckpointAdapter
                          |
                    +-----+-----+
                    |           |
                 memory       files
\endcode

The controller and adapters borrow their dependencies. The application owns
the ODESolver, TimeDependentOperator, adapter, propagator, storage backend,
moving window, and reverse callback, and must keep them alive for the complete
controller operation.

The solution Vector is not assumed to be a complete restart. CheckpointState
therefore stores the Vector, an explicit TimePoint, the continuation step size,
and opaque solver restart bytes. Forward Euler has no persistent stage history,
so its restart payload is empty and Restore reinitializes the solver.

Vector values are read through Vector::HostRead(), so checkpoint capture may
synchronize device data to host. Restore writes through Vector::HostWrite().
No asynchronous or device-resident storage is provided.

Persistent payloads use a 64-byte, versioned, canonical little-endian header.
Times, step sizes, and vector entries use IEEE binary64. Conversion to and from
single-precision real_t is exact for values originally captured from an MFEM
single-precision build. Opaque restart bytes follow the vector data. Decoding
rejects unsupported versions, malformed lengths, ID mismatches, truncation,
and trailing bytes.

FileCheckpointStorage uses one deterministic file per logical checkpoint. An
overwrite is first written and closed in a same-directory temporary file and
then renamed. A failed rename preserves the prior canonical file. Temporary
files are ignored and no fsync or crash-durability guarantee is made.

The following concepts correspond to the standalone checkpoint library:

\code{.unparsed}
Standalone                       MFEM
StepId / CheckpointId            StepId / CheckpointId
Snapshot                         Snapshot
MemoryStorage / FileStorage      MemoryCheckpointStorage / FileCheckpointStorage
SnapshotWindow<Vector>           ExactCheckpointWindow
StoreEverythingSchedule          StoreEverythingSchedule
RevolveSchedule                  RevolveSchedule
WangMoinIaccarinoSchedule        WangMoinIaccarinoSchedule
IntegratorCheckpointAdapter      ODESolverCheckpointAdapter
ForwardPropagator<Vector>        ODECheckpointPropagator
CheckpointExecutionController    CheckpointController
ReverseStepHandler               ReverseStepHandler
\endcode

Checkpoint schedules are independent of storage and numerical physics.
CheckpointController supplies exact predecessor and successor states to the
application-owned ReverseStepHandler; it does not define adjoint mathematics.
Example 42 demonstrates a discrete adjoint of the Forward Euler map and checks
it against a full-trajectory reference.
*/
